# XCU 2.3-token-recognition
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       An operator character that is not quoted ends the token before it
           and starts an operator token, so operators need no blanks around
           them: ; && || | ( ) and > all work when written tight against
           words, and a brace group closes with a semicolon written tight.
test       t/001-operators-delimit.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       Characters that can be joined into a longer operator are read as
           that operator: >> appends rather than being two redirections, ;;
           ends a case item, && and || are the list operators, << starts a
           here-document and >& is one operator.
test       t/002-operators-longest.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       Unquoted blanks (spaces and tabs) end a token and are discarded,
           however many there are.
test       t/003-blanks-delimit.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       An unquoted hash that begins a word starts a comment that runs to
           the end of the line, without removing the newline; a hash inside
           a word, or a quoted or escaped one, is an ordinary character.
test       t/004-comments.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       The end of quoted text does not end the token: quoted and
           unquoted pieces written together form one word.
test       t/005-quoting-not-delimiter.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       A word made of quoted and unquoted pieces, or ending in a
           substitution, is one token, and its quotes are removed only
           afterwards; a command name can therefore be written with quotes
           inside it.
test       t/006-token-continues.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       To find the end of a $( ) the shell tokenizes its text, so a
           closing parenthesis that is quoted, escaped, or the end of a case
           pattern does not end the substitution.
test       t/007-cmdsub-recursive-tokens.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
since      2024
says       The shell's tokenizing of the text of a $( ) recognizes a
           comment, so a closing parenthesis inside a comment there does not
           end the substitution.
test       t/008-cmdsub-comment-paren.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       After a here-document operator the rest of that line is read as
           ordinary tokens and kept; the body is taken from the lines after
           the next newline, and the saved tokens are then run; with two
           operators on a line the first body comes first.
test       t/009-heredoc-token-order.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       Each complete command is executed before the next one is
           tokenized and parsed, so a command that precedes a syntax error
           still runs.
test       t/010-execute-before-parse.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3.1
class      required
says       An alias defined by one command is in effect for the commands
           read after it completes: a following line that uses it is
           substituted.
test       t/011-alias-takes-effect.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3.1
class      required
says       A word in command-name position that names an alias is replaced
           by the alias value, which is read as new input (it can hold
           several commands); a word that is only an argument is not
           replaced; an alias value ending in a blank makes the next word
           eligible too.
test       t/012-alias-substitution.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3.1
class      required
says       A word containing any quoting is not replaced by an alias, and
           alias definitions are not inherited by a separate invocation of
           the shell.
test       t/013-alias-quoted-and-inherited.sh

assertion  XCU.2.3-token-recognition.014
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3.1
class      required
says       A word produced by replacing an alias is not replaced again by
           the same alias, so an alias may name the very command it expands
           to.
test       t/014-alias-not-recursive.sh
